ntfs-3g (ntfs-macos) patch 0016: INDEX_ROOT.index_block_size must be a power of two from 512 to 4096 bytes; a larger or odd value is rejected with EIO

Rationale (follow-up of F8, tests/fuzz/TRIAGE.md, finding F9): in NTFS-3G 2026.9.28 the on-disk field
INDEX_ROOT.index_block_size (layout.h, offset 8 of the $INDEX_ROOT value) is checked only for "at least 512"
and "a power of two", and not at all in some places. Every directory or index operation then allocates, reads
or writes blocks of that size, so a crafted directory makes one allocation of up to 2 GiB per open of the
directory (a power of two up to 2^31; 2^31 is also a negative int in some uses). Users of the field in the
library (all of libntfs-3g; the ntfsprogs tools other than ntfsfix and mkntfs are not shipped):
  - attrib.c:3547 ntfs_attr_inconsistent(): the consistency check of an INDEX_ROOT at the MFT record read
    (mft.c:289); minimum and power of two, no maximum. It runs once per MFT record read; the users below
    read the value again from the attribute and check as well.
  - dir.c:289 ntfs_inode_lookup_by_name() and dir.c:1196 ntfs_readdir() (each does ntfs_malloc(index_block_size),
    at dir.c:380 and dir.c:1283): minimum and power of two, no maximum. ntfs_readdir() also uses the size as a
    shift (ffs()) and a mask.
  - index.c:829 ntfs_index_lookup(): icx->block_size, which sizes every ntfs_malloc() of an index block of
    the index context (index.c:874, 1555, 1838, 1892, 2127), the block read (index.c:91, 732) and ntfs_ib_alloc();
    only a minimum is checked.
  - index.c:1138 ntfs_ir_to_ib(): ntfs_ib_alloc(ib_vcn, ib_size) of the on-disk size when an index root is moved
    to a new index block; no check.
  - dir.c:1587 sets a new index root from vol->indx_record_size; it is not a read and needs no change (a
    volume that passes 0015 has at most 4096 there).
Resource use (an allocation of up to 2 GiB per directory open of an untrusted volume), not a memory fault.
Real volumes: Windows and mkntfs write index blocks of 4096 bytes (the INDX record size of the boot sector is
the same; ntfs-3g mkntfs writes index_block_size = indx_record_size for every index it creates). The Linux
ntfs3 driver bounds the INDX record size at MAXIMUM_BYTES_PER_INDEX (4096).
Change (one hunk each in include/ntfs-3g/layout.h, include/ntfs-3g/index.h, libntfs-3g/attrib.c, dir.c, index.c):
  - New macro NTFS_MACOS_MAX_INDEX_BLOCK_SIZE (4096) in layout.h, the same value as NTFS_MACOS_MAX_RECORD_SIZE of 0015.
  - New function ntfs_index_block_size_valid(block_size) in index.c (declared in index.h): true when the size is
    at least NTFS_BLOCK_SIZE (512), at most 4096 and a power of two. It does not compare the size with the INDX
    record size of the boot sector: upstream never needs the two to be equal, and a mismatch on the root
    directory would fail the whole mount (ntfs_device_mount() calls ntfs_open_secure(), which looks up the
    root). The test is done in u32 without shift or arithmetic, so nothing overflows.
  - ntfs_index_lookup(), ntfs_inode_lookup_by_name() and ntfs_readdir(): the existing minimum and power of two
    test is replaced (dir.c, in as many lines as before, so that no later line of dir.c moves: the UBSan
    location dir.c:399 of known.tsv row U stays) or followed (index.c) by the function; an invalid value logs an
    error with ntfs_log_error() and fails with EIO before any allocation (dir.c goes to the existing EIO exit;
    index.c sets err = EIO). Callers that map a failed lookup to another errno keep doing so:
    ntfs_pathname_to_inode() reports ENOENT after ntfs_inode_lookup_by_name() fails, as it does for the
    existing "Corrupt directory" exits of dir.c.
  - ntfs_ir_to_ib(): same check; invalid: error log, EIO, NULL.
  - ntfs_attr_inconsistent(): an index_block_size above 4096 is "Corrupt index root", EIO, at the MFT record
    read. This is the check that a normal read reaches first: an INDEX_ROOT with a bad size fails to load with
    EIO, so the dir.c and index.c checks are defence in depth that is not reached once a record has passed
    this one (they read the same value from the same record). It is the only check that rejects 0 to 511 and
    non powers of two for every attribute; the three-way test there is unchanged. F9 on the patched engine
    therefore exercises this MFT record rejection. A bad root of the root directory, $Secure and the like
    fails the mount (as a corrupt root index record already did); a bad root of another directory makes that
    directory unreadable. There is no rule tied to the boot sector INDX record size, so a volume whose roots
    differ from it still mounts as before. No lower bound beyond the existing 512 is added.
Scope: library-wide and cross-platform (no __APPLE__ guard). It only adds rejections; every volume whose
index_block_size is a power of two from 512 to 4096 takes the same path as before. A volume with a larger
value is no longer readable through that index; none is known to exist.
Upstream status: not yet reported; draft in docs/UPSTREAM_REPORTS.md (F9); carried per ADR-026 until a fixed
release is pinned, then removed.
Test: ctest fuzz.findings.F9 replays tests/fuzz/image/findings/F9-index-root-block-size-alloc.bin through
fuzz_replay_mountwalk, registered only for an ASan build of the patched library (tests/fuzz/image/
CMakeLists.txt); the replay sets index_block_size of the root directory to 1 GiB and the allocation limit
set in the test (512 MB) makes the 1 GiB request in dir.c fail on the unpatched library
(allocation-size-too-big). The campaign classifies the signature as F9 in tests/fuzz/campaign/known.tsv:
seen on an engine that carries 0016 it is a REGRESSION.

